From 9b937caef9dc3aac05d9a30d944cd1e70b3b9fce Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ygecesefa <ygecesefa@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 22 Oct 2026 21:18:49 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] Decrypt /data: Samsung Weaver (hermesd, K250A) and the SPU

Android 15 wraps user 0's synthetic password with Weaver even without a
lock screen.  On the Galaxy Tab S9 Wi-Fi Weaver is Samsung's hermesd
(K250A secure element + SPU), so recovery brings the SPU up the way
Android does: sec_nvm and spdaemon, oneshot (the SPU starts once per
boot), with a tmpfs copy of the NVM from Android's persist partition
(mounted ro,noload, then unmounted: nothing is written to it).

- vendor/etc/ueventd.rc: mem_buf_vm nodes under /dev/mem_buf_vm/, where
  libvmmem looks for them; without them sec_nvm's buffer was never given
  to the SPU, its watchdog bit and the kernel panicked.
- hermesd with init's hermes_secnvm socket, logs in /tmp/hermes; no
  K250A firmware images in the ramdisk, so it can never update the
  secure element.
- the Weaver HIDL manifest fragment from the tablet's vendor.

Tested live in recovery (by hand, same steps): Weaver key read, spblob
unwrapped, "User 0 Decrypted Successfully", /data/media/0 readable.
